\subsection{Verifiable Searchable Encryption and Attribute-Based Search}

Verifiable searchable encryption (VSE) enables users to
verify the correctness and integrity of outsourced search
results, while attribute-based searchable encryption (ABSE)
provides fine-grained access control. Early
ABSE schemes~\cite{ref6} introduced attribute-constrained
search, which was later extended with hierarchical
search~\cite{ref36}, hidden access
policies~\cite{ref37}, multi-keyword
search~\cite{ref5,ref38,ref7},
blockchain-assisted auditability~\cite{ref39,ref40},
revocation~\cite{ref41,ref42}, and resistance to
keyword-guessing and leakage
attacks~\cite{ref43,ref44,ref45}. Recent VSE
schemes~\cite{ref13,ref14,ref15,ref16,ref17,ref35}
further support authenticated query processing through
result verification, secure delegation, provenance
validation, and blockchain-assisted integrity assurance.
Dynamic searchable encryption enhances multi-query
search~\cite{ref8}, forward/backward
privacy~\cite{ref46,ref47}, update
privacy~\cite{ref19,ref20}, and
privacy-preserving trapdoor
generation~\cite{ref18,ref19}. Nevertheless, existing
schemes remain predominantly keyword-oriented and do not
jointly support authenticated conjunctive numerical range
search with privacy-preserving encrypted aggregation.

\subsection{Privacy-Preserving Range Query}

Privacy-preserving range-query processing has been widely
studied for encrypted databases and IoT systems.
Order-preserving and order-revealing
encryption~\cite{ref48} enables efficient ciphertext
comparison but inherently leaks ordering information.
Encrypted range-query
schemes~\cite{ref21,ref20,ref22} improve retrieval for
numerical and spatial data but provide limited support for
conjunctive multidimensional queries and encrypted
analytics, while learned-index
approaches~\cite{ref15} mainly optimize indexing
efficiency rather than integrating access control,
aggregation, or authenticated verification.

Recent studies improve the scalability of encrypted range
search. Volume-Hiding
SSE~\cite{ref23} reduces retrieval leakage, Revocable
DSSE~\cite{ref24} supports dynamic healthcare search,
Scheme~\cite{ref25} enables efficient multidimensional
range queries, and Trinity~\cite{ref26} introduces
forward-secure spatio-temporal search using SHVE and
quotient filters. Although these schemes improve search
efficiency and expressiveness, they treat retrieval as an
independent task without integrated encrypted aggregation
or authenticated result verification.

More recent efforts combine range queries with
attribute-based and post-quantum searchable encryption.
ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27} supports attribute-authorized
numerical range queries but incurs increasing trapdoor
complexity as the query range expands. Lattice-based
searchable encryption~\cite{ref28} provides post-quantum
security under LWE assumptions but incurs substantial
lattice-computation overhead, limiting practicality for
resource-constrained IIoT deployments.

\subsection{Searchable Encryption with Privacy-Preserving Aggregation}

Homomorphic encryption enables computation directly over
encrypted data and has recently been integrated with
searchable encryption for secure cloud analytics. The
verifiable searchable encryption
framework~\cite{ref13} combines partial homomorphic
encryption, B$+$-Tree indexing, bitmap filtering, and
blockchain-assisted verification for Boolean search.
SEaaS~\cite{ref34} integrates searchable encryption with
homomorphic analytics, while
Refs.~\cite{ref18,ref19,ref20} improve query privacy,
update privacy, and multi-user searchable encryption.

However, existing schemes typically optimize search,
encrypted aggregation, or verifiable query processing in
isolation rather than providing a unified solution.
Encrypted aggregation is generally performed as a separate
post-processing step, introducing additional computation
and communication overhead. Moreover, existing
verification mechanisms primarily ensure retrieval
correctness without simultaneously supporting aggregation
integrity, authenticated completeness, freshness, and
rollback resistance.

Table~\ref{tab:feature_comparison} summarizes the
functional capabilities of representative schemes.
Verifiable searchable encryption
schemes~\cite{ref13,ref16,ref17} provide authenticated
search, but Refs.~\cite{ref13,ref16} do not support
numerical range queries, while Ref.~\cite{ref17} lacks
encrypted aggregation. Range-query
schemes~\cite{ref23,ref24,ref25,ref26,ref27,ref28}
improve retrieval efficiency but generally treat search,
aggregation, and verification independently. Conversely,
SEaaS~\cite{ref34} supports encrypted aggregation but
lacks conjunctive range search, fine-grained access
control, and verifiable query processing.

In contrast, BVCRSA introduces a query-centric
architecture that cryptographically binds authenticated
hierarchical range-cover indexing, protected bitmap
filtering, bitmap-aware encrypted aggregation, and
blockchain-assisted query-state verification within a
single authenticated query workflow. Consequently, it
enables efficient conjunctive range search, fine-grained
access control, privacy-preserving aggregation, and
authenticated query-state integrity, canonical-node
completeness, freshness, and rollback resistance for
outsourced IIoT analytics.


\section{Our Proposed Scheme}
\subsection{System Model}
\label{subsec:system_model}

BVCRSA comprises seven entities, as shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:system_model}. 

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{IIoT Sensor Nodes.}
Sensors collect industrial measurements, derive searchable
canonical-node metadata, encrypt records with record-specific
AES-GCM keys, encrypt aggregation values using Threshold
EC-ElGamal, and authenticate packets before transmission to
the Edge Gateway.

\item \textbf{Edge Gateway.}
The honest-but-curious, non-colluding gateway authenticates
sensor packets, assigns bitmap positions, constructs the
policy-bound range index and protected bitmap blocks,
registers aggregation metadata, and anchors signed epoch
commitments on the blockchain. It observes canonical-node
memberships but neither decrypts records nor participates in
threshold decryption.

\item \textbf{Cloud Server.}
The cloud stores encrypted records, searchable indexes,
protected bitmaps, and aggregation metadata. It evaluates
ABSE tokens, returns authenticated bitmap blocks, and
homomorphically aggregates user-selected records. Malicious
omission, modification, substitution, or replay attacks are
detected through Merkle proofs, blockchain commitments, and
independent aggregation verification.

\item \textbf{Blockchain Network.}
The permissioned blockchain stores signed epoch commitments
that authenticate searchable-index and aggregation states,
providing integrity, freshness, finality, and rollback
resistance without storing encrypted records or indexes
on-chain.

\item \textbf{Data User.}
An authorized user generates epoch-bound ABSE tokens,
verifies index and bitmap proofs, reconstructs authorized
bitmap blocks, evaluates the conjunctive query, and verifies
the encrypted aggregation before requesting threshold
decryption or authorized record retrieval.

\item \textbf{Threshold Decryption Authorities (TDAs).}
Each of the $n$ TDAs holds one Threshold EC-ElGamal secret
share. At least $t$ TDAs generate verifiable partial
decryptions, which are combined only after successful query
authentication.

\item \textbf{Trusted Authority (TA).}
The TA acts only during setup, generating public and master
parameters, issuing attribute keys, provisioning sensor and
gateway credentials, and distributing Threshold EC-ElGamal
shares. It does not participate in indexing, query
processing, aggregation, or verification after
initialization.

\end{enumerate}

