\subsection{Threat Model}

We consider probabilistic polynomial-time (PPT) adversaries that
attempt to infer sensor or query information, violate access
policies, recover aggregate plaintexts, or omit, modify, fabricate,
or replay query responses.

\subsubsection{Honest-but-Curious Edge Gateway}

The Edge Gateway faithfully authenticates sensor packets,
constructs the range index and protected bitmap blocks,
registers aggregation metadata, and anchors signed epoch
commitments. It may infer information from its authorized
view, including sensor identities, update times, searchable
dimensions, canonical-node memberships, and bitmap
positions. Although it holds
$K_{\mathrm{sel}}$, it possesses neither the ABSE master
secret, user attribute keys, record decryption keys, nor any
Threshold EC-ElGamal share, and therefore cannot decrypt
records or aggregate ciphertexts.

The gateway is assumed not to collude with the cloud.
Collusion could reveal protected bitmap contents and access
patterns, although record and aggregate plaintexts remain
cryptographically protected. A malicious gateway that
constructs or commits an incorrect index is outside the
threat model; verification is therefore relative to the
gateway-committed state.

\subsubsection{Malicious Cloud Server}

The cloud is privacy-curious and malicious with respect to
query execution. It observes encrypted records, ABSE
tokens, protected bitmap blocks, aggregation metadata,
access patterns, and the leakage
$\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BVCRSA}}$. It may omit matching
nodes, modify protected blocks, substitute aggregation
entries, return incorrect aggregates, or replay previous
responses.

Such attacks are detected through signed blockchain
commitments, Merkle proofs, aggregation multi-proofs,
epoch-bound query tokens, and independent homomorphic
recomputation. The cloud cannot decrypt records or
aggregates, generate valid tokens for unauthorized
policies, or forge authenticated query states except with
negligible probability. Availability against a nonresponsive
cloud is outside the scope of this work.

\subsubsection{Unauthorized and Colluding Users}

Unauthorized users may attempt to generate valid ABSE
tokens, recover unauthorized keys, replay tokens, or
collude with other users or the cloud. ABSE keys are
assumed collusion-resistant, preventing unauthorized policy
satisfaction through key combination. Query tokens are
bound to the finalized epoch, query identifier, and
expiration time to mitigate replay and substitution attacks.

\subsubsection{Threshold Decryption Authorities}

Each TDA holds one Threshold EC-ElGamal share. An
adversary may corrupt fewer than $t$ of the $n$ TDAs and
collude with the cloud or users, but cannot reconstruct the
decryption key or recover aggregate plaintexts. Malformed
partial decryptions are detected using DLEQ proofs.
Collusion among at least $t$ TDAs and TDA refusal attacks
are outside the scope of this work.

\subsubsection{Blockchain and Deployment Assumptions}

The permissioned blockchain provides authenticated access,
consensus finality, and immutable finalized states. Users
accept only the finalized epoch referenced by the query and
verify its gateway signature, timestamp, and validity
period. Consequently, modifying or rolling back a finalized
state requires breaking blockchain finality, the signature
scheme, or the collision resistance of the hash function.

The TA is trusted only during initialization to generate
system parameters, issue credentials, and distribute
threshold shares. After setup, it does not participate in
indexing, query processing, aggregation, or verification.
Cryptographic primitives are assumed secure, nonces are
never reused, and communication channels are authenticated.
Compromise of the TA or sensors, a malicious gateway,
gateway-cloud collusion, corruption of at least $t$ TDAs,
rewriting finalized blockchain states, and physical,
side-channel, or denial-of-service attacks are outside the
scope of this work.

